# Wunder Predictorium — LOB Price Movement Forecasting

Solution scaffolding for the **Wunder Predictorium** challenge by Wunder Fund: predict two future
price-movement indicators (`t0`, `t1`) from sequences of Limit Order Book + trade states.
See `PROJECT_README.md` for the official rules and `docs/dominio-lob.md` /
`docs/metrica.md` for the domain background.

> Competition website: https://wundernn.io/predictorium

---

## Quick start

```bash
# One-shot environment setup (~5-10 min — downloads CUDA wheels).
bash scripts/install_env.sh
conda activate lob

# Install the project in editable mode so `lob ...` / `python -m pipeline.cli ...` work anywhere.
pip install -e . --no-deps

# All tests should pass on a fresh install.
pytest

# Two equivalent ways to run the pipeline:
lob list                                                  # entry point
python -m pipeline.cli train --config configs/models/lightgbm.yaml
python -m pipeline.cli train --config configs/models/gru.yaml
```

### Single-shot deliverable (`main.ipynb`)

The grading-ready notebook lives at the repo root:

```bash
jupyter lab main.ipynb     # or: jupyter notebook main.ipynb
```

It covers the full narrative — introduction, EDA, feature engineering, three rounds of
model iterations (classical / DL / SOTA), model selection and submission packaging — and
runs on top of the same `src/` pipeline so anything you change in the codebase is
reflected the next time the notebook is executed. A top-level `QUICK_MODE` flag controls
whether it trains in ~10–20 min (default, on a subset) or in the full ~hours budget.

The notebook is regenerated from a single Python source:

```bash
python scripts/make_notebook.py   # writes main.ipynb
```

The CLI writes everything to `experiments/<model_name>/<timestamp>/`:

```
experiments/gru/gru__20260515-153000/
├── config.yaml                # config used for this run
├── meta.json                  # run metadata (best score, kind, …)
├── model.pt                   # weights / state_dict
├── solution.zip               # ready-to-upload submission
├── train_history.csv          # per-epoch metrics
├── train_history.png          # loss + val correlation
├── submission_pkg/            # unzipped contents of solution.zip
└── val/
    ├── metrics.json           # weighted Pearson + MSE + MAE
    ├── predictions.parquet    # raw predictions for downstream analysis
    ├── scatter.png            # pred vs true scatter (per target)
    └── per_seq_corr.png       # histogram of per-sequence correlation
```

Once a run is packaged you can replay it through the official scorer:

```bash
python -m pipeline.cli score --run experiments/gru/gru__20260515-153000
```

